CAT = "ccrnGiRenal"; SEG = "GRI"
Q = [
("Measuring intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) in mm Hg, which finding defines abdominal compartment syndrome?",
 "IAP over 20 with organ failure", ["IAP of 12 on a single reading", "IAP of 5 with abdominal pain", "IAP of 8 with distension"],
 "Abdominal compartment syndrome is a sustained intra-abdominal pressure above 20 mm Hg with new organ dysfunction, such as falling urine output or rising airway pressures. A pressure of 12 mm Hg or more is intra-abdominal hypertension."),
("How is intra-abdominal pressure usually measured at the bedside?",
 "Through the urinary bladder", ["Through a rectal probe", "Through a central venous line", "Through an arterial line"],
 "A small volume of saline is instilled into the bladder through the urinary catheter and the pressure is read with the transducer zeroed at the iliac crest in the midaxillary line, with the patient supine at end-expiration."),
("A patient with cirrhosis is vomiting bright red blood and esophageal varices are suspected. Which drug infusion should the nurse expect?",
 "Octreotide", ["Heparin", "Labetalol", "Nitroprusside"],
 "Octreotide lowers portal pressure by reducing splanchnic blood flow and is started before endoscopic band ligation. A nonselective beta blocker such as propranolol is used later to prevent rebleeding, not in the acute bleed."),
("Besides vasoactive drugs and endoscopy, which therapy lowers mortality in a patient with cirrhosis and a GI bleed?",
 "Prophylactic antibiotics", ["An albumin 25% bolus", "A platelet transfusion", "A lactulose retention enema"],
 "Patients with cirrhosis who bleed are at high risk of bacterial infection, and short-course prophylactic antibiotics such as ceftriaxone reduce infection, rebleeding and death."),
("A patient with cirrhosis is confused with asterixis. Which drug is first-line?",
 "Lactulose", ["Loperamide", "Lorazepam", "Morphine"],
 "Lactulose traps ammonia in the colon and speeds its removal; it is titrated to two or three soft stools a day, often with rifaximin. Benzodiazepines and opioids worsen hepatic encephalopathy, and loperamide works against lactulose."),
("Which sign suggests hypocalcemia in a patient with acute pancreatitis?",
 "Trousseau sign", ["Cullen sign", "Murphy sign", "Grey Turner sign"],
 "Carpal spasm when a BP cuff is inflated (Trousseau) or facial twitching when the facial nerve is tapped (Chvostek) suggest low calcium, which occurs in pancreatitis as calcium binds to fat necrosis. Cullen and Grey Turner signs are bruising from retroperitoneal bleeding."),
("A patient with moderately severe acute pancreatitis cannot eat. Which nutrition plan is preferred?",
 "Early enteral feeding", ["NPO for seven days", "Parenteral nutrition", "Clear liquids for weeks"],
 "Enteral feeding started early protects the gut barrier and lowers infection and organ failure compared with parenteral nutrition, which is kept for patients who cannot be fed enterally."),
("An older patient with atrial fibrillation has severe abdominal pain, but the abdomen is soft with little tenderness. Lactate is 5.2 mmol/L. What should the nurse suspect?",
 "Mesenteric ischemia", ["Viral gastroenteritis", "Early appendicitis", "A peptic ulcer"],
 "Pain out of proportion to the examination, especially with atrial fibrillation as an embolic source, is the classic picture of acute mesenteric ischemia. A rising lactate suggests bowel infarction and needs urgent imaging and surgery."),
("After blunt abdominal trauma, a patient reports pain in the left shoulder. Which organ is most likely injured?",
 "The spleen", ["The liver", "The right kidney", "The appendix"],
 "Kehr sign is left shoulder pain from blood irritating the diaphragm, referred along the phrenic nerve, and it is classic for splenic injury. Liver injury more often refers pain to the right shoulder."),
("After an esophagectomy, the patient's nasogastric tube comes partly out. What should the nurse do?",
 "Notify the surgeon, do not reinsert", ["Push it back to the original mark", "Remove the tube completely now", "Flush it firmly with normal saline"],
 "The tube lies across a fresh anastomosis, and blind repositioning can perforate it. The surgeon decides on replacement, usually under fluoroscopy or endoscopy."),
("A malnourished patient starts tube feeding after two weeks without eating. Which electrolyte should be watched most closely in the first days?",
 "Phosphorus", ["Sodium", "Chloride", "Total calcium"],
 "Refeeding drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells as insulin rises, and a sharp fall in phosphate can cause weakness, respiratory failure and heart failure. Feeding is started slowly with close checks and replacement."),
("Which method confirms that a blindly placed feeding tube is in the stomach before first use?",
 "An abdominal x-ray", ["Auscultating an air bolus", "The color of the aspirate", "Bubbles in a cup of water"],
 "A radiograph showing the whole tube is the standard for confirming placement before feeding or medication. Listening for an air bolus cannot tell the stomach from the lungs and has led to feeding into the airway."),
("A patient has a fractional excretion of sodium of 0.5% and a urine sodium of 12 mmol/L. What does this suggest?",
 "Prerenal azotemia", ["Acute tubular necrosis", "Postrenal obstruction", "Interstitial nephritis"],
 "A FENa below 1% means the tubules are working and holding sodium in response to low perfusion, which points to a prerenal cause. In ATN damaged tubules waste sodium, so FENa is usually above 2%."),
("Which urine finding is most typical of acute tubular necrosis?",
 "Muddy brown granular casts", ["Red blood cell casts", "White blood cell casts", "Hyaline casts and nothing else"],
 "Sloughed tubular cells form muddy brown granular casts in ATN. Red cell casts point to glomerulonephritis, white cell casts to pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis, and hyaline casts alone fit a prerenal state."),
("Which drug is nephrotoxic and is dosed by serum level monitoring?",
 "Vancomycin", ["Ceftriaxone", "Pantoprazole", "Acetaminophen"],
 "Vancomycin and aminoglycosides can injure the kidney and are dosed by levels; risk rises with contrast, NSAIDs and hypotension. The other drugs listed do not need level monitoring for nephrotoxicity."),
("When is continuous renal replacement therapy preferred over intermittent hemodialysis?",
 "When the patient is unstable", ["When potassium must fall fast", "When the patient is walking", "When access is a mature fistula"],
 "CRRT removes fluid and solutes slowly over 24 hours, which hemodynamically unstable patients tolerate better. Intermittent hemodialysis removes potassium faster and suits stable patients."),
("A patient on CRRT is anticoagulated with citrate. Which lab must be checked regularly?",
 "Ionized calcium", ["Platelet count", "Serum amylase", "Serum troponin level"],
 "Citrate binds calcium to stop the circuit clotting, and calcium is replaced to keep the patient's ionized calcium normal. Citrate build-up, seen as a rising total-to-ionized calcium ratio, must be watched for."),
("A sacral pressure injury shows full-thickness skin loss with visible fat but no exposed bone, tendon or muscle. What stage is it?",
 "Stage 3", ["Stage 2", "Stage 4", "Deep tissue injury"],
 "Stage 3 is full-thickness skin loss with subcutaneous fat visible. Stage 4 exposes fascia, muscle, tendon or bone, and stage 2 is partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis."),
("A patient has severe leg pain out of proportion to a small red area, with crepitus and rising heart rate. What is the priority treatment?",
 "Surgical debridement", ["Warm compresses", "IV antibiotics alone", "Elevation and rest"],
 "Necrotizing fasciitis spreads along fascial planes faster than the skin shows, and only urgent surgical removal of dead tissue with broad-spectrum antibiotics controls it. Antibiotics alone do not reach necrotic tissue."),
("A peripheral IV running norepinephrine has infiltrated, and the skin is pale and cool. Which antidote is usually injected into the area?",
 "Phentolamine", ["Hyaluronidase", "Heparin flush", "IV naloxone"],
 "Phentolamine, an alpha blocker, reverses the local vasoconstriction from vasopressor extravasation. Hyaluronidase is used for other vesicants and hypertonic fluids. The infusion is stopped and moved first."),
]
